<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">camp</ansi>

On the road, your company can stop and make camp instead of pushing on.
A camp is real: it stays put while you're logged in or off, and it
survives a restart. Nothing about it advances the world clock or the
round count; a rest takes real time, not game time.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Making camp</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">camp</ansi>
  Makes camp in this room, if the ground here allows it. You can only
  have one camp at a time; if you already have one, this just points you
  at <ansi fg="command">camp status</ansi>.

  <ansi fg="command">camp fire</ansi>
  Lights the campfire. You need a lit fire before you can rest. The
  fire needs fuel: in a room that offers firewood the deadfall feeds it for
  nothing; anywhere else it burns one firewood bundle from your pack, a
  companion's pack or the cargo. A damp bundle lights only on a second try
  and gives light but no warmth, unless you carry fire steel and tinder
  (<ansi fg="command">help camp gear</ansi>). With no firewood there is no fire and no
  rest at camp. One bundle fuels one rest: when a rest finishes the fire
  burns down to embers, which keep the camp warm until you break it, but
  resting again means feeding the fire with <ansi fg="command">camp fire</ansi> once more. Making camp and <ansi fg="command">camp status</ansi> show the fuel your company
  carries. Markets sell bundles (they never buy them back), and you can
  gather your own (<ansi fg="command">help gathering</ansi>).

  A camp is part of the room: <ansi fg="command">look</ansi> shows it to everyone
  there, a cold fire pit until the fire is lit and bedrolls around a
  crackling campfire after, glowing embers once it has burned down, and
  the tent when one is pitched (an oiled canvas tent, a fur-lined tent...). Someone else's camp is shown with its
  leader's name. It goes when the camp is struck.

  <ansi fg="command">camp status</ansi>
  Shows your camp, whether the fire is lit, how a rest is progressing,
  and your company's current needs, with each member's rest duty once you
  have set any.

  <ansi fg="command">camp duties</ansi>
  Lets members work through the next rest instead of sleeping: keep watch,
  tend the company's wounds and blades, forage, cook or brew (<ansi fg="command">help camp duties</ansi>).
  Everyone sleeps by default, and the rest is unchanged unless you set a duty.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Resting</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">camp rest</ansi>
  Settles your company in by the fire for one real-time rest, about a
  minute. While resting you can't use ordinary exits, though you can
  still talk and use most non-movement commands; <ansi fg="command">camp status</ansi> or
  <ansi fg="command">look</ansi> shows how far along it is.

  When the rest finishes, every current company member's Fatigue rises
  by 20 (less if bad weather is spoiling the rest, see below), and you
  and every companion with you gain the <ansi fg="yellow">Rested</ansi> buff for 15 minutes of
  real time (not anyone you gave a rest duty: <ansi fg="command">help camp duties</ansi>). Rested cuts your walking strain. If anyone already has the
  better <ansi fg="yellow">Well Rested</ansi> buff from an inn stay (<ansi fg="command">help inn</ansi>), the camp rest
  still restores their Fatigue but doesn't touch that buff — Well Rested
  always wins, and a camp rest never replaces it with the lesser Rested.
  A companion who wasn't with you when the rest finished still gets its
  share of Rested once it rejoins, for whatever time is left.

  A finished rest also knits wounds with what the company carries, using
  it up (<ansi fg="command">help wounds</ansi>): a birch splint for each broken bone, and a
  linen bandage for each cut or puncture:
  <ansi fg="command">Your broken arm has knit. (wound healed; a splint used)</ansi>. Without them
  a camp heals no wounds: that takes an inn or a physician. Then the
  finished rest restores you and every companion with you to full health
  (up to each wound limit) and full mana, once per rest; a rest that
  raiders break restores neither
  (<ansi fg="command">help readiness</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help mana</ansi>).

  The same rest brews flasks: every Alchemist's satchel in your company is refilled
  from your reagents, one a flask (<ansi fg="command">help alchemist</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help brew</ansi>).

  Foul weather makes for a poorer rest: the Fatigue you recover is
  scaled down (never increased) by how bad conditions are where you've
  camped, so the same rest recovers less in a storm than under clear
  skies. In a shelter room (<ansi fg="command">help resources</ansi>) the penalty is halved: recovery
  moves halfway from the weather's figure toward a full rest; so does a tent
  (a fur-lined tent shuts the weather out altogether).
  After a rest the fire is embers, so another rest costs another bundle.
  Camp gear improves a rest: bedrolls add a quarter more Fatigue for each
  member who has one, a tent shelters the company (and trades something
  for something: <ansi fg="command">camp tent</ansi>), bells and trip lines
  warn of raiders and keep thieves out, and a field surgeon's kit treats a
  wound (<ansi fg="command">help camp gear</ansi>). The rest report lists the gear in use.
  On dangerous roads a camp without bells may be robbed while it sleeps; you
  are told what is missing when you wake.

  <ansi fg="command">camp poison</ansi>
  Keeps a preparation list of which weapon poison goes on which blade, and
  coats the whole company's blades from it in one go (<ansi fg="command">help poisons</ansi>). Nothing
  is spent until <ansi fg="command">camp poison apply</ansi>, and it applies everything or nothing.

  <ansi fg="command">camp sharpen</ansi>
  Spends your whetstones sharpening your company's blades, the same as
  the <ansi fg="command">sharpen</ansi> command. See <ansi fg="command">help sharpen</ansi> for how that works, including
  <ansi fg="command">sharpen auto on</ansi>, which sharpens automatically whenever a camp rest
  ends.

  <ansi fg="yellow-bold">Before you sleep</ansi>
  Sharpening, poisoning and cooking are camp chores: do them before the
  rest begins. In the web client the Camp tab lists them under "Before you
  sleep", each with a button, who does it, and what it uses; a chore with
  nothing to do says why, such as "No blades are dull enough to need the
  whetstone." Fortifying broth and watch incense join the list when you
  carry them (camp prepare broth all, camp prepare incense). Once the
  company sleeps the chores close; sharpen, camp cook and camp poison
  apply refuse until the rest ends. Poison and sharpening work anywhere
  you stand still; cooking needs a lit fire.

  When a camp rest finishes, gear you are wearing reveals what it is, and
  your best Scribe reads the unidentified gear the company carries
  (<ansi fg="command">help identify</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Breaking camp</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">camp break</ansi>
  Strikes the camp. You can't break camp while resting; wait for the
  rest to finish (or let it lapse) first. Breaking camp doesn't refund
  the rest, change your hunger or thirst, or move you anywhere.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Usage:</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">camp</ansi> | <ansi fg="command">camp status</ansi> | <ansi fg="command">camp fire</ansi> | <ansi fg="command">camp rest</ansi> | <ansi fg="command">camp cook</ansi> | <ansi fg="command">recipes</ansi> | <ansi fg="command">camp break</ansi>
  <ansi fg="command">camp tent</ansi> [<ansi fg="command">canvas</ansi>|<ansi fg="command">fur</ansi>|<ansi fg="command">camouflaged</ansi>|<ansi fg="command">large</ansi>|<ansi fg="command">clear</ansi>]
  <ansi fg="command">camp duties</ansi> [member|all] [<ansi fg="command">sleep</ansi>|<ansi fg="command">watch</ansi>|<ansi fg="command">tend</ansi>|<ansi fg="command">forage</ansi>|<ansi fg="command">cook</ansi>|<ansi fg="command">brew</ansi>] | <ansi fg="command">camp duties clear</ansi>
  <ansi fg="command">camp poison</ansi> [<ansi fg="command">assign</ansi>|<ansi fg="command">unassign</ansi>|<ansi fg="command">preview</ansi>|<ansi fg="command">apply</ansi>]
  <ansi fg="command">camp sharpen</ansi> [<ansi fg="command">status</ansi> | <ansi fg="command">auto on</ansi>|<ansi fg="command">off</ansi>]
  <ansi fg="command">camp music</ansi> [<ansi fg="command">on</ansi>|<ansi fg="command">off</ansi>]

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Cooking, raids, and camp specialists</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">camp cook</ansi> [ingredient ...]
  With your camp's fire lit, cook a dish you know from what your pack and
  the company cargo hold, or name ingredients to try a new mix
  (<ansi fg="command">help cooking</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help recipes</ansi>).

  <ansi fg="command">camp supplies</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">camp prepare</ansi> [supply] [member]
  Fortifying broth, a warming draught, a cooling salve, or watch incense,
  prepared at the camp before a rest or a hard road (<ansi fg="command">help camp supplies</ansi>).

Camping in the wilds isn't always safe: raiders may come during a rest
(<ansi fg="command">help campwatch</ansi>). A finished rest that raiders didn't spoil also
brings a ranger's forage (<ansi fg="command">help forage</ansi>) and a cleric's vigil
(<ansi fg="command">help vigil</ansi>).

Musicians can play a song as a rest begins: longer Rested, more Fatigue, a
quicker first battle, faster recovery from ailments, at the price of a
likelier raid (<ansi fg="command">help music</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help instruments</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">camp music on</ansi>|<ansi fg="command">off</ansi>).
At an inn the same company can play for pay (<ansi fg="command">help gigs</ansi>).

Your companions talk as a rest begins and ends, and the web client's Camp tab
shows their latest words (<ansi fg="command">help banter</ansi>).

In the web client, Company > Camp shows your camp and a rest's progress,
with buttons for what you can do right now (<ansi fg="command">help webclient</ansi>).

The web client's map also marks your camp as a tent on its tile, with its
fire and a sleeping mark while you rest. The camps of your own party show
as blue tents; no other company's camp is ever shown (<ansi fg="command">help worldmap</ansi>).

See also: <ansi fg="command">help music</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help instruments</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help gigs</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help camp duties</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help camp gear</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help camp supplies</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help specialists</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help inn</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help sharpen</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help poisons</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help survival</ansi>,
<ansi fg="command">help weather</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help conditions</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help travel</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help banter</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help events</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help opinions</ansi> (what your companions think of a rough camp), <ansi fg="command">help bonds</ansi> (a rest together draws companions closer, or not).

A successful Camp Watch also prevents enemy surprise in a raid. Perception can still prevent surprise after a missed watch, but does not restore spoiled rest. See help ambush.
